{
  "title": "Refitting changes forecast readout error across fitted encoders",
  "caption": "Wall-position MAE at horizon eight in the prespecified current-wall-unrevealed evaluation cohort: 716 contexts (8.7% of 8,192 sampled starts) have a wall already present but its gap unrevealed, and continue without reset to a wall-present future. This is a survival-conditioned descriptive subset, not the full-cohort primary analysis. All three encoder seeds and both predefined train-only normalizations are shown. R\u2192R fits/evaluates a linear readout on real future encodings; R\u2192F transfers that real-feature readout to forecast features; F\u2192F fits/evaluates the same readout class on forecast features. Copy fits/evaluates on the current real latent without future actions. Hollow markers use the common current-training-latent scaler; filled markers use source/horizon-specific training scalers, retained unchanged during transfer. The dashed reference is the original training-mean wall-position predictor evaluated on this cohort, not a mean refit to the subset. The dash-dot line adds a separately labeled post-hoc interpretation check: the constant median wall position computed from the corresponding 1,509 TRAINING-cohort labels, with no model or feature-probe refit. Its evaluation MAE is 4.642 px; the training-cohort mean also achieves 4.769 px. Both outperform the seed-2 adapted forecast readout (6.164 px) and real-feature positive control (6.531 px). This independently verified check supplements rather than replaces the originally declared global-mean baseline. Whiskers use 2,000 evaluation-stream bootstrap draws, conditional on each fitted model and readout; they do not quantify model-training uncertainty. In seed 2, adaptation reduces MAE by 3.85 px [3.48,4.22] under per-source normalization and 3.89 [3.52,4.25] under common normalization. Its F\u2192F-minus-Copy interval includes zero in both analyses (-0.63 [-1.41,0.13] and -0.64 [-1.42,0.12], respectively). This reduction in MAE does not by itself establish wall tracking or information recovery: calibration to the selected subset can reduce error relative to the global training-mean baseline without tracking variation within the subset. It also does not establish superior forecasting relative to Copy. A separately labeled post-hoc interpretation audit finds seed-2 F\u2192F position correlation -0.175 in this slice; in the full wall-present cohort its correlation is +0.246 but RMSE is 18.65 px versus 13.49 px for the fixed training-mean baseline. Thus the displayed MAE reduction cannot certify reliable information recovery. Seeds 0 and 1 have much weaker real-feature positive controls. All other horizons and cohorts remain available in the source analysis. Supervised labels are used only for this frozen-model diagnostic and never enter model training, control inputs or reward.",
